# Development Rules & Framework Architecture

## Core Philosophy
- Prioritize vanilla, highly decoupled architectures.
- Keep components small, single-purpose, and predictable.
- Avoid unnecessary external dependencies; leverage native web standards where possible.

## Tech Stack Specifics
- Frontend/Hosting: Vercel deployment standards.
- Backend/Database: Supabase (utilize robust row-level security, efficient indexing, and clean relational schemas).
- Version Control: Maintain clean, atomic git commits.

## Code Generation Guidelines
- Always write clean, self-documenting TypeScript code.
- Ensure strict type safety and comprehensive error handling.
- Do not add placeholders, boilerplate, or "TODO" comments unless explicitly asked.

## Style Guide
- **Button labels:** Use Title Case for headlines, buttons, and similar UI chrome (e.g. `Save Settings`, not `Save settings`).
- **Button text must not wrap:** Primary, secondary, and danger buttons use `white-space: nowrap` so labels like `Save Settings` stay on one line. Size buttons to their text (`width: fit-content`) plus horizontal padding — not full width unless a specific layout requires it.
- **Admin notices:** Success — light green background, dark green border and text. Error — pink background, 2px dark red border, dark red text.
- **Admin action buttons:** Add/save actions often use the green gradient (`.admin-blog-add-button`). Bulk delete — red background, white text, centered. Publish icon green; delete icon red.
- **Admin table Actions columns:** Icon-only buttons only — no text labels inside the cell (use `aria-label` for accessibility). Primary actions (edit, save, cancel) stay visible. The delete icon is hidden until row hover or keyboard focus within the row, then appears instantly (no animation delay). Hovering the delete icon shows an instant tooltip labeled `Delete` (Title Case). Keep the Actions column sticky on wide horizontal-scroll tables.
- **Poll pods:** Module panel backgrounds are transparent by default; pod styling comes from Poll Settings. Header pill uses configurable colors (default header background `#5acff9`, dark turquoise ink). Keep header `border-radius: 999px` only when height is pill-like; taller headers should read as rounded boxes. ~100px gap between Current Poll and Previous Results pods. Question/content width is left-aligned, not centered.
- **Pods container:** Site “pod” panels use white or configured backgrounds, rounded corners, alignment with main body content.
- See `docs/look-and-feel-compiled.md` for the full list of user look-and-feel notes from prior sessions (review and merge into this section over time).

# Normie Project — Cursor Rules & Coding Conventions

## Stack
- Next.js 15 (App Router) with TypeScript
- Supabase (PostgreSQL) for the database
- Tailwind is NOT used — all styling is custom CSS in `app/globals.css`
- Tiptap (v3.22.5) for rich text editing — all tiptap packages must stay pinned to 3.22.5
- No component library (no shadcn, no MUI, etc.)

## Project Structure
```
app/                        # Next.js App Router pages and API routes
  admin/                    # Admin-only routes (protected)
  api/                      # API route handlers
  globals.css               # ALL styles live here — no CSS modules, no Tailwind
components/
  builder/                  # Page builder editor components
    builder-module-card.tsx         # Module editor UI (all module types)
    builder-section-card.tsx        # Row/section editor UI
    builder-background-controls.tsx
    builder-utils.ts                # Style helpers: getHeadingModuleStyle, etc.
    builder-types.ts                # Module palette types
  builder-template-preview.tsx      # Live renderer for builder pages
  builder-rich-text-editor.tsx      # Tiptap rich text editor
  admin-builder-editor.tsx          # Main builder orchestrator (state + handlers)
src/
  site/
    layout/
      site-shell.tsx          # Page wrapper (no hardcoded nav — builder owns it)
      site-nav.tsx            # Navigation component
    dynamic/
      dynamic-page-shell.tsx  # Renders builder pages via BuilderTemplatePreview
    home/                     # Legacy home page (only used if no builder "home" page)
lib/
  builder-template.ts         # Core types: BuilderTemplateModule, BuilderTemplateSection, etc.
  admin-media.ts
supabase/                     # DB schema and migrations
```

## Page Builder Architecture

### How pages render
1. `app/page.tsx` checks for a builder page with slug `"home"` in the DB
2. If found → `DynamicPageShell` → `BuilderTemplatePreview` (builder owns everything)
3. If not found → legacy `HomePageView` fallback
4. All other routes follow the same pattern via their respective `page.tsx` files

### Builder data flow
- `AdminBuilderEditor` holds all state (`draft`, `expandedModuleIds`, `collapsedSectionIds`, `cellModules`)
- State flows down through `BuilderSectionCard` → `BuilderModuleCard`
- All mutations go through `updateSection()` and `updateModule()` helpers
- `cloneModule`, `cloneSection`, `saveModule`, `saveCellModules` follow the same pattern
- Module settings are always `Record<string, string>` — never typed sub-objects

### Module types
`heading`, `text`, `quote`, `button`, `image`, `navigation`, `headline-rotator`, `table`, `slider`, `social`, `contact-form`, `previous-results`, `current-poll`

### Navigation module
- `NavigationModulePreview` in `builder-template-preview.tsx` renders from `module.settings.navItems` (JSON array of `{id, label, href}`)
- It does NOT hardcode logo, Login link, or nav items — those are fully builder-controlled
- The site shell (`site-shell.tsx`) does NOT render any nav — the builder owns the header entirely

### Adding a new prop to a module card
Always touch all three files in order:
1. `builder-module-card.tsx` — add to `BuilderModuleCardProps` type + destructure + use
2. `builder-section-card.tsx` — add to `BuilderSectionCardProps` type + destructure + pass to `<BuilderModuleCard>`
3. `admin-builder-editor.tsx` — implement the handler function + pass as prop to `<BuilderSectionCard>`

## TypeScript Conventions
- Strict mode is on — no `any` unless absolutely unavoidable
- Module settings are always `Record<string, string>` — parse JSON inside the component
- Use `crypto.randomUUID()` for new IDs on cloned sections/modules
- Use `Date.now()` based IDs for cloned modules within sections (existing pattern)
- Prefer explicit return types on exported functions
- Use `void` prefix when calling async functions in event handlers: `onClick={() => void saveTemplate()}`

## CSS Conventions
- All styles in `app/globals.css` — no inline styles except for dynamic builder values
- CSS variables defined in `:root` — use `var(--accent)`, `var(--ink)`, `var(--muted)` etc.
- Builder-specific classes prefixed with `builder-`
- Site shell classes prefixed with `site-shell-`
- Admin classes prefixed with `admin-`
- Never use `!important` unless overriding third-party styles
- The `.site-nav` class controls the nav bar appearance globally

## API Routes
- All admin API routes live under `app/api/admin/`
- Cell modules API: `GET/POST /api/admin/cell-modules`, `DELETE/PATCH /api/admin/cell-modules/[id]`
- Media API: `GET /api/admin/media`, `POST /api/admin/media` (file upload)
- Pages: `GET/POST /api/admin/pages`, `PATCH/DELETE /api/admin/pages/[id]`
- Templates: `GET/POST /api/admin/page-templates`, `PATCH/DELETE /api/admin/page-templates/[id]`
- Public `GET /api/polls/next` accepts optional `startPoll` (poll UUID) plus optional `category` to show that published poll as the current question (used by Poll Manager View).
- All API responses return `{ data?, error? }` shape

## Rich Text Editor (Tiptap)
- All tiptap packages pinned to `3.22.5` — do not upgrade
- Custom extensions: `FontSizeStyle`, `TextShadowStyle`, `TextOutlineStyle`, `LineHeightStyle`
- Code view toggle: `isCodeView` state + `formatHTML()` for pretty-printing
- Always use `--legacy-peer-deps` when installing tiptap-related packages

## Heading Module Styles
Computed in `getHeadingModuleStyle()` in `builder-utils.ts`:
- Drop shadow: `4px 4px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5)` (lower-right, 2px blur)
- Outline: `2px ${color}` via `WebkitTextStroke`
- Underline color matches text color via `textDecorationColor`

## Saved Modules / Cell Repository
- Saved cells and modules use the same API endpoint (`/api/admin/cell-modules`)
- A saved module is a `BuilderCellModuleRecord` with a single item in `modules[]`
- A saved cell is a `BuilderCellModuleRecord` with all modules from a column in `modules[]`
- `cellModules` state in `AdminBuilderEditor` holds all saved records
- `insertCellModule()` clones modules with fresh IDs before inserting

## Cloning Pattern
All clone functions follow this pattern:
```ts
function cloneSomething(id: string) {
  setDraft((c) => {
    const idx = c.items.findIndex((item) => item.id === id);
    if (idx < 0) return c;
    const cloned = { ...c.items[idx], id: crypto.randomUUID() };
    const arr = [...c.items];
    arr.splice(idx + 1, 0, cloned);
    return { ...c, items: arr };
  });
}
```

## Common Pitfalls
- Never set `showNav` prop on `SiteShell` — it no longer exists
- Never render hardcoded logo or Login link in `NavigationModulePreview`
- Don't assume the homepage uses `HomePageView` — it uses the builder if a "home" page exists in DB
- `npm install` must always use `--legacy-peer-deps` due to tiptap peer dependency conflicts
- Clear `.next` cache (`rm -rf .next`) after corrupted builds or `node_modules` reinstall
- Disk space below 20GB causes `.next` build artifacts to disappear mid-session
- The project is at `~/WebApps/normie` (moved from `~/Desktop/WebApps/normie`)
- Supabase free tier pauses after 7 days of inactivity — resume from dashboard if connections time out

## Builder Preview vs Live Site
- `/preview` uses `BuilderTemplatePreview` directly — not wrapped in `SiteShell`
- Live pages go through `DynamicPageShell` → `SiteShell` → `BuilderTemplatePreview`
- Changes to `SiteShell` affect live pages only, not `/preview`
- The preview page has its own shell with Browser/Mobile toggle and Back to Builder link

## Privacy & Legal
- `/privacy` and `/terms` pages exist and are linked in the `SiteShell` footer
- Core data commitment: individual user data is NEVER sold — only aggregate anonymized data
- This must be reflected in any user-facing copy or data handling code

